ne place (e.g., a data structure mapping markers to commands/descriptions), not scattered across two functions.\n\n---\n\n### FRICTION 10: Sibling Module Sourcing Pattern Scaled Without Centralization\n**Module:** Scattered across `lib/core.sh:69-77` + every module that sources siblings  \n**Issue:** The idiom `if ! declare -F fn >/dev/null 2>&1; then source file; fi` is used to idempotently source files and guard against re-execution. Two entry points:\n- `lib/core.sh:_almanac_source_sibling` helper (for modules that source core.sh first)\n- Literal inline pattern (for modules like `lib/run.sh` that have no core.sh dependency)\n\nEvery module that uses the pattern states its own invariant: \"if function X exists, assume this file is already loaded.\" This works but scales poorly: adding a new shared utility that lib/foo.sh and lib/bar.sh both want requires both to add the same idempotency check, and if either forgets the guard, the second source re-defines all functions.  \n**Friction:** The dependency graph is implicit in the guard functions. To understand the sourcing order, you must trace through each module's header (the comments state which deps it needs). There's no place that documents the module graph. A change to the order (e.g., lib/agent.sh now needs to source lib/role.sh first) requires grep'ing for all places that source both and potentially re-ordering their includes. The helper in core.sh exists to consolidate the pattern, but not every module uses it (those without core.sh dependency inline the check), so the pattern exists in two forms across the codebase.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThese 10 findings cluster into three themes:\n\n1. **Shallow seams masking complexity:** The adapter seams (loops, providers, roles) are clean abstract interfaces, but their implementations duplicate logic (discovery), hide contracts in globals (argv), or defer critical decisions (health calculation) to callers.\n\n2. **Data structure pluralism:** Multiple representations 