hrough the\\n#     agent capture shape, recording the worker's status.tsv / events.jsonl /\\n#     result.txt under the run dir, and marks it done with the agent's exit code\\n#   - almanac_loop_worker_watch: streams one worker's live event log (and reports\\n#     cleanly when no log exists yet)\\n# The run registry / worker-health read-views live in lib/run.sh (tests/test-run.sh).\\n\\nset -euo pipefail\\n\\nROOT=\\\"$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")/..\\\" && pwd)\\\"\\nsource \\\"$ROOT/lib/worker.sh\\\"\\n\\nTMPDIRS=()\\nNEW_TMPDIR=\\\"\\\"\\n\\ncleanup() {\\n  local dir\\n  [ \\\"${#TMPDIRS[@]}\\\" -eq 0 ] && return 0\\n  for dir in \\\"${TMPDIRS[@]}\\\"; do\\n    rm -rf \\\"$dir\\\"\\n  done\\n}\\ntrap cleanup EXIT\\n\\nfail() {\\n  echo \\\"  FAIL: $1\\\" >&2\\n  exit 1\\n}\\n\\nassert_eq() {\\n  local expected=\\\"$1\\\" actual=\\\"$2\\\" message=\\\"$3\\\"\\n  [ \\\"$expected\\\" = \\\"$actual\\\" ] || fail \\\"$message (expected '$expected', got '$actual')\\\"\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\",\"uuid\":\"b58e70ad-c544-4c62-8b4d-f62d1031eb5d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:39:57.371Z\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"task_description\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_progress\",\"task_id\":\"abd0a901e249bd220\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"description\":\"Reading lib/converge-core.sh\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"usage\":{\"total_tokens\":81835,\"tool_uses\":29,\"duration_ms\":41170},\"last_tool_name\":\"Read\",\"uuid\":\"ddd767fa-e1db-4002-a6cd-fd569e135d27\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\",\"id\":\"msg_01DGb6Dmaqh7coCV3udKA5Jg\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_0144y7qXUHza5YmfoD28ePUD\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/converge-core.sh\",\"offset\":230,\"limit\":120},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1214,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":80524,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":1214,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":3,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\",\"uuid\":\"653a8437-2534-4e10-929f-7a2b3954ae37\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CbShEARPcaxP8rqGFpM96\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"task_description\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_0144y7qXUHza5YmfoD28ePUD\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"230\\t\\n231\\talmanac_converge_overseer_parse() {\\n232\\t  local input=\\\"${1:-}\\\"\\n233\\t  local content line key_line current_key=\\\"\\\"\\n234\\t  local raw_verdict=\\\"\\\" raw_reason=\\\"\\\" raw_steer=\\\"\\\" raw_goal=\\\"\\\"\\n235\\t  local found_verdict=0 found_reason=0 found_steer=0 found_goal=0\\n236\\t\\n237\\t  if [ -f \\\"$input\\\" ]; then\\n238\\t    content=\\\"$(cat \\\"$input\\\")\\\"\\n239\\t  else\\n240\\t    content=\\\"$input\\\"\\n241\\t  fi\\n242\\t\\n243\\t  ALMANAC_CONVERGE_VERDICT=\\\"CONTINUE\\\"\\n244\\t  ALMANAC_CONVERGE_REASON=\\\"\\\"\\n245\\t  ALMANAC_CONVERGE_STEER=\\\"none\\\"\\n246\\t  ALMANAC_CONVERGE_GOAL_UPDATE=\\\"unchanged\\\"\\n247\\t\\n248\\t  # State-machine line walker. Each KEY: prefix switches the current field;\\n249\\t  # subsequent non-key lines append to whatever field is currently open.\\n250\\t  #\\n251\\t  # Pre-leniency: parser only matched the literal \\\"KEY:\\\" prefix. LLMs that\\n252\\t  # added markdown bold (`**VERDICT:**`), heading hashes (`### VERDICT:`),\\n253\\t  # or list-bullet markers (`- VERDICT:`) silently failed the case match,\\n254\\t  # found_X stayed 0, and the guard at the bottom returned with all defaults\\n255\\t  # — producing the canonical \\\"VERDICT=CONTINUE, REASON='', STEER=none,\\n256\\t  # GOAL_UPDATE=unchanged\\\" mirage seen across every overseer tick of three\\n257\\t  # consecutive converge runs.\\n258\\t  #\\n259\\t  # Now: strip a small set of decorative prefixes (markdown bold, leading\\n260\\t  # `#`/`-`/`*`/`>` characters + space) from the start of each line BEFORE\\n261\\t  # the case match, and trim trailing `**` for the bold-close variant\\n262\\t  # (`**VERDICT:**`). This is purposely a tight whitelist — we don't want\\n263\\t  # to start accepting `; VERDICT:` or `// VERDICT:` and then debug new\\n264\\t  # ambiguities; just the markdown shapes models actually emit.\\n265\\t  while IFS= read -r line || [ -n \\\"$line\\\" ]; do\\n266\\t    # Normalize a small set of decorative prefixes that wrap KEY: markers.\\n267\\t    # The targeted shapes (in order, all anchored to start of line):\\n268\\t    #   `**VERDICT:** value`   markdown bold + colon-close\\n269\\t    #   `**VERDICT:**`         bold-only, value on next line\\n270\\t    #   `### VERDICT: value`   markdown heading (1-6 hashes)\\n271\\t    #   `- VERDICT: value`     list bullet (`-` or `*`)\\n272\\t    #   `> VERDICT: value`     quote marker (rare but seen)\\n273\\t    # Each strips ONLY the surrounding decoration, leaves the KEY: literal\\n274\\t    # alone so the case match below works unchanged. Continuation lines\\n275\\t    # (paragraph body) bypass these substitutions when they don't start\\n276\\t    # with a recognized decoration — values keep their internal markdown\\n277\\t    # (e.g. a REASON paragraph with `**bold**` inline emphasis stays\\n278\\t    # intact).\\n279\\t    key_line=\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\"$line\\\" | sed -E '\\n280\\t      s/^[[:space:]]+//\\n281\\t      s/^\\\\*\\\\*([A-Z_]+):\\\\*\\\\*[[:space:]]*/\\\\1: /\\n282\\t      s/^\\\\*\\\\*([A-Z_]+):\\\\*\\\\*[[:space:]]*$/\\\\1:/\\n283\\t      s/^#+[[:space:]]+([A-Z_]+:)/\\\\1/\\n284\\t      s/^[-*][[:space:]]+([A-Z_]+:)/\\\\1/\\n285\\t      s/^>[[:space:]]*([A-Z_]+:)/\\\\1/\\n286\\t    ')\\\"\\n287\\t    case \\\"$key_line\\\" in\\n288\\t      VERDICT:*)\\n289\\t        current_key=\\\"VERDICT\\\"; found_verdict=1\\n290\\t        raw_verdict=\\\"${key_line#VERDICT:}\\\"\\n291\\t        case \\\"$raw_verdict\\\" in \\\" \\\"*) raw_verdict=\\\"${raw_verdict# }\\\" ;; esac\\n292\\t        ;;\\n293\\t      REASON:*)\\n294\\t        current_key=\\\"REASON\\\"; found_reason=1\\n295\\t        raw_reason=\\\"${key_line#REASON:}\\\"\\n296\\t        case \\\"$raw_reason\\\" in \\\" \\\"*) raw_reason=\\\"${raw_reason# }\\\" ;; esac\\n297\\t        ;;\\n298\\t      STEER:*)\\n299\\t        current_key=\\\"STEER\\\"; found_steer=1\\n300\\t        raw_steer=\\\"${key_line#STEER:}\\\"\\n301\\t        case \\\"$raw_steer\\\" in \\\" \\\"*) raw_steer=\\\"${raw_steer# }\\\" ;; esac\\n302\\t        ;;\\n303\\t      GOAL_UPDATE:*)\\n304\\t        current_key=\\\"GOAL_UPDATE\\\"; found_goal=1\\n305\\t        raw_goal=\\\"${key_line#GOAL_UPDATE:}\\\"\\n306\\t        case \\\"$raw_goal\\\" in \\\" \\\"*) raw_goal=\\\"${raw_goal# }\\\" ;; esac\\n307\\t        ;;\\n308\\t      *)\\n309\\t        # Continuation line — append to whichever field is currently open.\\n310\\t        # Lines before the first KEY: marker (preamble noise) have current_key\\n311\\t        # empty and are dropped, matching the prompt's \\\"no preamble\\\" contract.\\n312\\t        # The original line (not the leading-whitespace-stripped key_line) is\\n313\\t        # preserved so multi-line paragraphs keep their indentation if any.\\n314\\t        case \\\"$current_key\\\" in\\n315\\t          VERDICT)     raw_verdict=\\\"${raw_verdict}\\\"$'\\\\n'\\\"${line}\\\" ;;\\n316\\t          REASON)      raw_reason=\\\"${raw_reason}\\\"$'\\\\n'\\\"${line}\\\" ;;\\n317\\t          STEER)       raw_steer=\\\"${raw_steer}\\\"$'\\\\n'\\\"${line}\\\" ;;\\n318\\t          GOAL_UPDATE) raw_goal=\\\"${raw_goal}\\\"$'\\\\n'\\\"${line}\\\" ;;\\n319\\t        esac\\n320\\t        ;;\\n321\\t    esac\\n322\\t  done <<< \\\"$content\\\"\\n323\\t\\n324\\t  # Partial-output policy: capture every field we DID find, default-fill the\\n325\\t  # rest. Pre-fix the parser bailed completely on any missing field —\\n326\\t  # silently returning all-defaults — which masked the LLM's actual\\n327\\t  # response. Now: VERDICT is required (a missing verdict can't be safely\\n328\\t  # defaulted to CONTINUE — that's confusing; bail), but REASON / STEER /\\n329\\t  # GOAL_UPDATE missing just keep their initial defaults (\\\"\\\", \\\"none\\\",\\n330\\t  # \\\"unchanged\\\") and we proceed with the verdict we have. The bare verdict\\n331\\t  # case (LLM emits \\\"VERDICT: CONTINUE\\\" followed by a paragraph instead of\\n332\\t  # the other three keys) now produces a useful tick: the verdict is\\n333\\t  # honored, the REASON section in overseer.log shows empty (operator can\\n334\\t  # tighten the prompt), STEER stays none, goal stays unchanged.\\n335\\t  if [ \\\"$found_verdict\\\" -ne 1 ]; then\\n336\\t    # No usable verdict at all — surface this so the operator sees the\\n337\\t    # parse failure in the overseer log instead of a silent all-defaults\\n338\\t    # fallback. Caller still gets the conservative CONTINUE default but\\n339\\t    # the parse-failure marker stamps the cause.\\n340\\t    ALMANAC_CONVERGE_PARSE_NOTE=\\\"no VERDICT marker found\\\"\\n341\\t    return 0\\n342\\t  fi\\n343\\t  ALMANAC_CONVERGE_PARSE_NOTE=\\\"\\\"\\n344\\t\\n345\\t  # Note in the parse marker which optional fields the LLM skipped — so the\\n346\\t  # operator can see whether to tighten the prompt for that model.\\n347\\t  local _missing=()\\n348\\t  [ \\\"$found_reason\\\" -eq 1 ] || _missing+=(REASON)\\n349\\t  [ \\\"$found_steer\\\" -eq 1 ]  || _missing+=(STEER)\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\",\"uuid\":\"0aedefcb-7ff2-4422-9c9a-04bf760768b9\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:40:03.125Z\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"task_description\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_progress\",\"task_id\":\"abd0a901e249bd220\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"description\":\"Reading lib/harden-core.sh\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"usage\":{\"total_tokens\":81838,\"tool_uses\":30,\"duration_ms\":41452},\"last_tool_name\":\"Read\",\"uuid\":\"2866582f-f2b7-40f4-9b14-4112e03b9186\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\",\"id\":\"msg_01DGb6Dmaqh7coCV3udKA5Jg\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01Cica5J1CZKTj26LCHNAqBv\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh\",\"offset\":500,\"limit\":100},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1214,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":80524,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":1214,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":3,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\",\"uuid\":\"9c968cb5-3a12-4a3b-8638-0bba724e3817\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CbShEARPcaxP8rqGFpM96\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"task_description\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01Cica5J1CZKTj26LCHNAqBv\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"500\\t\\n501\\t  if [ ! -f \\\"$path\\\" ]; then\\n502\\t    {\\n503\\t      printf '%s\\\\n\\\\n' \\\"# Findings Ledger\\\"\\n504\\t      printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"<!-- Append-only finding records managed by 'almanac harden'. One section per finding. -->\\\"\\n505\\t    } > \\\"$path\\\"\\n506\\t  fi\\n507\\t}\\n508\\t\\n509\\t# True when the ledger already holds a finding with this id.\\n510\\talmanac_harden_ledger_has() {\\n511\\t  local path=\\\"$1\\\"\\n512\\t  local id=\\\"$2\\\"\\n513\\t\\n514\\t  [ -f \\\"$path\\\" ] || return 1\\n515\\t  grep -Fxq -- \\\"## $id\\\" \\\"$path\\\"\\n516\\t}\\n517\\t\\n518\\t# Append one finding section. Returns 1 (and writes nothing) when the id is\\n519\\t# already present, so callers can count genuinely-new findings. Creates the\\n520\\t# ledger file with its header on first write.\\n521\\talmanac_harden_ledger_append_entry() {\\n522\\t  [ \\\"$#\\\" -ge 7 ] || return 2\\n523\\t\\n524\\t  local path=\\\"$1\\\"\\n525\\t  local id=\\\"$2\\\"\\n526\\t  local lens=\\\"$3\\\"\\n527\\t  local severity=\\\"$4\\\"\\n528\\t  local location=\\\"$5\\\"\\n529\\t  local claim=\\\"$6\\\"\\n530\\t  local demonstration=\\\"$7\\\"\\n531\\t  local status=\\\"${8:-open}\\\"\\n532\\t  local round=\\\"${9:-1}\\\"\\n533\\t  local adjudication=\\\"${10:-}\\\"\\n534\\t\\n535\\t  almanac_harden_ledger_init \\\"$path\\\"\\n536\\t\\n537\\t  if almanac_harden_ledger_has \\\"$path\\\" \\\"$id\\\"; then\\n538\\t    return 1\\n539\\t  fi\\n540\\t\\n541\\t  almanac_harden_ledger_append_entry_unchecked \\\\\\n542\\t    \\\"$path\\\" \\\"$id\\\" \\\"$lens\\\" \\\"$severity\\\" \\\"$location\\\" \\\"$claim\\\" \\\\\\n543\\t    \\\"$demonstration\\\" \\\"$status\\\" \\\"$round\\\" \\\"$adjudication\\\"\\n544\\t}\\n545\\t\\n546\\t# Append one already-deduped finding section. Callers must have initialised the\\n547\\t# ledger and decided the id is new; this keeps bulk ingestion from grepping the\\n548\\t# growing ledger once per finding.\\n549\\talmanac_harden_ledger_append_entry_unchecked() {\\n550\\t  [ \\\"$#\\\" -ge 7 ] || return 2\\n551\\t\\n552\\t  local path=\\\"$1\\\"\\n553\\t  local id=\\\"$2\\\"\\n554\\t  local lens=\\\"$3\\\"\\n555\\t  local severity=\\\"$4\\\"\\n556\\t  local location=\\\"$5\\\"\\n557\\t  local claim=\\\"$6\\\"\\n558\\t  local demonstration=\\\"$7\\\"\\n559\\t  local status=\\\"${8:-open}\\\"\\n560\\t  local round=\\\"${9:-1}\\\"\\n561\\t  local adjudication=\\\"${10:-}\\\"\\n562\\t\\n563\\t  {\\n564\\t    printf '\\\\n## %s\\\\n\\\\n' \\\"$id\\\"\\n565\\t    printf -- '- lens: %s\\\\n' \\\"$lens\\\"\\n566\\t    printf -- '- severity: %s\\\\n' \\\"$severity\\\"\\n567\\t    printf -- '- location: %s\\\\n' \\\"$location\\\"\\n568\\t    printf -- '- claim: %s\\\\n' \\\"$claim\\\"\\n569\\t    printf -- '- demonstration: %s\\\\n' \\\"$demonstration\\\"\\n570\\t    printf -- '- status: %s\\\\n' \\\"$status\\\"\\n571\\t    printf -- '- round: %s\\\\n' \\\"$round\\\"\\n572\\t    printf -- '- adjudication: %s\\\\n' \\\"$adjudication\\\"\\n573\\t  } >> \\\"$path\\\"\\n574\\t}\\n575\\t\\n576\\t# Normalize a reviewer's JSON-Lines result into ledger-entry TSV rows\\n577\\t# (id, lens, severity, location, claim, demonstration, status, round,\\n578\\t# adjudication). New findings are status=open with an empty adjudication.\\n579\\t# Malformed reviewer lines are dropped cleanly (via almanac_harden_findings_tsv).\\n580\\talmanac_harden_parse_findings() {\\n581\\t  local result_file=\\\"$1\\\"\\n582\\t  local round=\\\"${2:-1}\\\"\\n583\\t  local lens severity location claim demonstration id\\n584\\t\\n585\\t  while IFS=$'\\\\t' read -r lens severity location claim demonstration; do\\n586\\t    [ -n \\\"${lens}${severity}${location}${claim}${demonstration}\\\" ] || continue\\n587\\t    id=\\\"$(almanac_harden_finding_id \\\"$lens\\\" \\\"$location\\\" \\\"$claim\\\")\\\"\\n588\\t    printf '%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\t%s\\\\n' \\\\\\n589\\t      \\\"$id\\\" \\\"$lens\\\" \\\"$severity\\\" \\\"$location\\\" \\\"$claim\\\" \\\"$demonstration\\\" \\\"open\\\" \\\"$round\\\" \\\"\\\"\\n590\\t  done < <(almanac_harden_findings_tsv \\\"$result_file\\\")\\n591\\t}\\n592\\t\\n593\\t# Parse a reviewer result and append each new finding to the ledger, deduping\\n594\\t# against everything already adjudicated. Prints the count of findings actually\\n595\\t# added (duplicates do not count).\\n596\\talmanac_harden_ledger_record() {\\n597\\t  local path=\\\"$1\\\"\\n598\\t  local result_file=\\\"$2\\\"\\n599\\t  local round=\\\"${3:-1}\\\"\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\",\"uuid\":\"c4a35d76-5eda-4c36-98b3-55449accf92e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-05-27T07:40:03.410Z\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\",\"task_description\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_notification\",\"task_id\":\"abd0a901e249bd220\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"output_file\":\"\",\"summary\":\"Explore almanac codebase friction\",\"usage\":{\"total_tokens\":85510,\"tool_uses\":30,\"duration_ms\":87946},\"uuid\":\"70bf1a70-591b-46d0-9565-7ac87cfe5aef\",\"session_id\":\"5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01TT2qPdF9rLAHNrvVUCFZTN\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Perfect. Now I have enough information to write a comprehensive friction analysis. Let me compile my findings:\\n\\n## Architectural Friction Report: Almanac Bash Loop Engine\\n\\n### FRICTION 1: TSV Record Engine — Shallow Module with Complex Round-Trip Contract\\n**Module:** `lib/run.sh:75-108` (`almanac_loop_tsv_record_set`)  \\n**Issue:** The generic TSV record engine reads the entire canonical field list, then enumerates it line-by-line to round-trip existing values + apply overrides. This design enforces the schema by construction — a good principle — but the implementation is complex for what it does: reads all fields from file, checks every new pair against the schema, then rewrites every field. Callers name only fields they have, yet the engine iterates the entire schema twice (validation pass, then rewrite).  \\n**Friction:** Two wrappers (`almanac_loop_record_set`, `almanac_loop_worker_record_set`) delegate to the same deep function with their own field lists. The generic function is harder to test in isolation (requires a full file round-trip) than the schema lists that feed it. Real bugs hide in the round-trip logic (missing field preservation, blank-value preservation) not in the schema enforcement. Interface is nearly as complex as its implementation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 2: Global Argv Variables as Cross-Module Contract\\n**Module:** `lib/agent.sh` & `lib/loops/ralph.sh`  \\n**Issue:** Provider and loop adapters populate global arrays `_ALMANAC_AGENT_ARGV` and `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` to communicate exec commands back to callers. No return value — just a side effect on a module-level variable. The three agent shapes (`capture`, `stream`, `raw`) all call `_almanac_agent_build` → reads `_ALMANAC_AGENT_ARGV` into a local array. Similarly, the launcher calls loop adapters' `exec_argv` verb expecting them to populate `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV`, then immediately uses it.  \\n**Friction:** State hidden in globals makes the interface implicit and harder to reason about. Callers must remember to read the argv after the verb returns; if they forget, they get a stale/empty value silently. No return codes signal success — the side effect is the contract. Tests must set up the globals; tracing where they're modified requires grepping for the global name.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 3: Converge Overseer Parser — Complex State Machine Masking LLM Failures\\n**Module:** `lib/converge-core.sh:231-350` (`almanac_converge_overseer_parse`)  \\n**Issue:** The 120-line state machine parses overseer output (VERDICT, REASON, STEER, GOAL_UPDATE markers) by normalizing markdown decorations (bold, headings, bullets, quotes) then matching on KEY: prefix. Originally, a missing VERDICT silently defaulted to CONTINUE — \\\"producing a canonical mirage across three consecutive converge runs.\\\" Now it flags parse notes but still auto-defaults REASON/STEER/GOAL_UPDATE when missing.  \\n**Friction:** The parser is doing two jobs — normalizing user-facing format variance AND recovering from LLM failures — in the same function. The sed regex (lines 279-286) is a whitelist of decorations but makes the output harder to reason about. The distinction between \\\"found no VERDICT\\\" (fatal, returns a parse note) and \\\"found VERDICT, missing REASON\\\" (non-fatal, defaults are used) is clear but the implementation conflates both paths. Understanding what verdicts the overseer can emit requires reading the prompt in `converge-core.sh:167+`, not the parser.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 4: Loop + Provider Seams Duplicate Adapter Discovery\\n**Module:** `lib/loops.sh:30-63` & `lib/agent.sh:36-68`  \\n**Issue:** Both seams implement identical adapter discovery: a glob loop over files in a subdirectory, basename extraction, source each file, list discovery. Two parallel implementations:\\n- `lib/loops.sh`: `almanac_loop_adapter_load` / `almanac_loop_adapter_list` / `almanac_loop_adapter_known`\\n- `lib/agent.sh`: `almanac_provider_load` / `almanac_provider_list` / `almanac_provider_known`\\n\\nEach has the same line-by-line logic: for f in dir/*.sh; name=$(basename f .sh); printf %s name.  \\n**Friction:** The duplication is not copy-paste (each evolved independently) but the contract is identical — it's a pattern that should exist once. Adding a third seam (e.g., runners, tutors, feedback detectors) means writing the same discovery three times. Real maintainability hit: if a future change standardizes loader behavior (e.g., dependency injection, caching), both places need updating.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 5: Worker Health Calculation Scattered Across lib/run.sh\\n**Module:** `lib/run.sh:512-577` (worker health helpers)  \\n**Issue:** Four helpers read worker status to compute health (running|idle|stalled|looping|failed|done):\\n- `almanac_loop_worker_health` (lines 512–537): reads status file → extracts pid/status → checks if pid is alive\\n- `almanac_loop_file_age_secs` (540–551): timestamps\\n- `almanac_loop_count_lines` (553–558): event-log line counts\\n- `almanac_loop_trailing_repeat` (560–574): detects \\\"output stalling\\\" (repeated last line for N secs)\\n\\nEach is a small pure function, but they're called from `almanac_loop_hub_render` (line 619+) to build dashboard frames. The health calculation is not a single place — the dashboard logic interleaves calls to these four helpers plus status-field reads to determine what glyph to render.  \\n**Friction:** No single function encapsulates \\\"what is this worker's health?\\\". To understand how health is computed, you must trace through `hub_render` and see it synthesize pid+status+event-log-age+output-staleness across multiple helper calls. Bugs hide in the order of checks (e.g., stalled vs idle), and adding a new health signal (e.g., cpu usage) requires editing the dashboard composer, not a health calculator.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 6: Role Config Resolution Buried in Env-Var Normalization\\n**Module:** `lib/role.sh:39-104` (`almanac_loop_role_field` + `almanac_loop_role_config`)  \\n**Issue:** Role config (provider/model/effort per ralph agent / harden conductor/reviewer/fixer) is resolved through a four-layer env-var cascade:\\n1. PREFIX_ROLE_LENS_FIELD (most specific)\\n2. PREFIX_ROLE_FIELD\\n3. PREFIX_FIELD (shared across roles)\\n4. default_value (fallback)\\n\\nThe resolver `almanac_loop_role_field` (5 args, 25 lines) composes candidate keys via `almanac_loop_env_key_part` (normalizes to uppercase, collapses non-alphanumeric to `_`). Harden and converge each write a thin wrapper (`almanac_harden_role`, `almanac_converge_role`) that calls the generic resolver with hardcoded prefix + default.  \\n**Friction:** The layer-cake is clear in the code comment but the contract is implicit in which args you pass and which defaults you supply. Each loop's wrapper hard-codes its role names (conductor/reviewer/fixer for harden; agent/overseer for converge) but the resolver is generic enough to handle any. Adding a fourth role to harden means a one-line addition to `harden-core`, but the resolution layers are defined in `role.sh` so you must understand BOTH files to see the full precedence. Tests for role resolution live in `tests/test-role.sh` but tests for harden-role-specific defaults live in harden tests — split across two test files.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 7: Hub Control Flow (stop/steer) Shares Signal-File Logic with Loops\\n**Module:** `lib/run.sh:778-830` (`almanac_loop_run_control_file` / `stop` / `steer`) calling `lib/loops.sh:118-128` (`almanac_loop_signal_file`)  \\n**Issue:** The hub writes stop/steer directives by calling `almanac_loop_signal_file(type, kind)` to get the basename (`.ralph-stop`, `.harden-steer`, …), then concatenating with root to form the full path. The loop runner reads the same files at its between-round checkpoint. This coupling is intentional — the seam ensures both sides speak the same signal-file language — but the orchestration logic lives in `run.sh` (the registry) while the dispatch to adapters lives in `loops.sh` (the seam).  \\n**Friction:** To understand how stop/steer control works end-to-end, you trace from `hub_render` (read state) → `run.stop` (write stop file) → `loops.signal_file` (resolve basename) → ralph's between-round check (read stop file). The path is clear but spans three modules. Adding a new control signal (e.g., `.ralph-pause` for checkpointing) requires changes in `loops.sh` (default signal file) AND in `run.sh` (the control functions that dispatch to it), plus the runner itself. It's not composable — you can't just add a new loop-agnostic control verb.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FRICTION 8: Harden Ledger (rubric/findings) — Three Layers of Similar Data Structures\\n**Module:** `lib/harden-core.sh:69-606` (rubric + ledger + finding TSV)  \\n**Issue:** Harden maintains three stores for findings:\\n1. **rubric.md** (prose, seeded once with goal/","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/jakubneumann/.claude/projects/-Users-jakubneumann-Documents-code-neumie-almanac/5a7c94be-b519-4ed0-b07c-6b329c049eda/tool-results/bttxfo6ne.txt","persistedOutputSize":243524}}
